# Patch 0062 — Kavacha's own pages follow the theme.
#
# SYMPTOM: select Kavacha Daylight and you get a light browser wrapped around
# four black pages — about:studio, about:marketplace, about:plugins and the
# new-tab dashboard. One of them is the Customization Studio you selected the
# theme from.
#
# CAUSE. All four are CONTENT documents. The --kavacha-* token floor lives in
# zen-theme.css, which is chrome, and a content document is not in that cascade.
# So every `var(--kavacha-surface, #14111f)` in studio.css, marketplace.css and
# plugins.css resolved to its literal fallback, always, and all three then
# pinned `color-scheme: dark` to match. newtab.css did not even reference a
# token: `color: #fff; background: #1c1d22`, hardcoded.
#
# Their own header comments claimed "the theme engine re-tints --kavacha-* live",
# which was true of chrome and never true of them. Patch 0046 diagnosed this
# exactly and deferred the fix: "Folding all of them into one shared content
# stylesheet is the real fix; it needs a new jar entry and moz.build wiring,
# which cannot be verified without a full build."
#
# WHY NOT THAT SHARED STYLESHEET. A static sheet cannot carry the values. The
# tokens are chosen at runtime from whichever package is active, including user
# and marketplace packages that did not exist at build time — a stylesheet could
# only hardcode one theme, which is the bug. So: a shared SCRIPT. All four pages
# are privileged (IS_SECURE_CHROME_UI for the three about: pages; the new tab is
# a chrome:// URL whose newtab.js already calls Services.prefs), so they can ask
# KavachaThemeEngine directly. content-theme.js stamps the resolved tokens,
# color-scheme and kavacha-theme-mode onto the document root and re-stamps on
# kavacha.theme.active. The pages keep their `var(--kavacha-*, literal)` chains
# untouched — the difference is that the var now resolves.
#
# It also fixes a second thing 0046 named: --zen-primary-color is a chrome
# token, so the documented
# `var(--zen-primary-color, var(--kavacha-accent, var(--kavacha-accent-fallback)))`
# chain fell to the fallback on all three about: pages. The accent a user picked
# in Settings was permanently invisible in the Studio. content-theme.js sets it.
#
# NEW JAR ENTRY: src/zen/kavacha-theme/jar.inc.mn, #include'd from
# zen-assets.jar.inc.mn. Contrary to 0046's expectation this does NOT need a
# full build — measured 2026-08-03, `./build/bootstrap.sh fast` (15s) picked the
# new entry up and it appeared in dist/ and in Kavacha.app.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT THEMED: the new-tab dashboard above its base layer. It is a
# photo backdrop, and .credit/.settings/.clock/.greeting/.quote are white with
# scrims and text-shadows measured against the IMAGE (see the contrast note at
# .scrim, and defect D2). Recolouring them from --kavacha-text-primary would put
# dark text on a dark photograph the moment you chose a light theme — the
# opposite of the fix. Only `background` moves; `color` stays #fff, because
# .clock and .greeting set no colour of their own and inherit it from body. A
# light theme gets a light browser around a photographic new tab, the way a
# light desktop keeps whatever wallpaper you chose.
#
# VERIFIED (Marionette, buildID 20260803), all four pages, both directions:
#   Daylight  -> mode light, color-scheme light, --kavacha-surface #F7F6FB,
#                body background rgb(247,246,251) on the three about: pages and
#                rgb(236,234,244) (the sunken token) on the new tab.
#   Midnight  -> mode dark, color-scheme dark, --kavacha-surface #0B0912,
#                body background rgb(11,9,18) / rgb(6,4,9).
#   Accent #4AA3DF set in Settings reaches --zen-primary-color on all four.
#
# FILES: src/zen/kavacha-theme/content-theme.js (new);
# src/zen/kavacha-theme/jar.inc.mn (new);
# src/browser/base/content/zen-assets.jar.inc.mn;
# src/zen/kavacha-studio/studio.{html,css};
# src/zen/kavacha-marketplace/marketplace.{html,css};
# src/zen/kavacha-plugins/plugins.{html,css};
# src/zen/kavacha-newtab/{index.html,newtab.css}.
